 **`focus()` on a window that lives on another desktop switches to that desktop first**, so focus never lands on a window the user cannot see. `HOOKS.DESKTOP_SWITCHED` fires before the window's own `WINDOW_FOCUSED`. `open()` never does this: a window opened onto an inactive desktop (session restore, a lazily loaded native window) joins it without taking focus, and the active desktop stays where it is.
 
+#### Keep on top
+
+A window can be **kept on top**: it stays above every window that is not, even while another window has focus or a new one opens. The user toggles it with the pin in the title bar (the `core/keep-on-top` control, before minimize); code calls `setAlwaysOnTop()` and reads the window's `alwaysOnTop` property.
+
+```javascript
+wp.os.windowManager.setAlwaysOnTop( 'my-plugin-timer', true );
+wp.os.windowManager.getById( 'my-plugin-timer' ).alwaysOnTop; // true
+```
+
+- **Several windows can be kept on top at once.** Among themselves they stack in normal focus order; letting one go drops it back to where focus order puts it among the rest.
+- **Focus is unchanged.** `getFocused()` still returns the window the user last picked, even while a kept-on-top window paints over it. A fullscreen window still covers everything.
+- **A child follows its owner.** The open children of a kept-on-top window are lifted with it, so an owner is never pinned above the child that blocks it.
+- **It is session state.** The flag is saved with the window (`alwaysOnTop: true` on its `SessionWindow` entry) and restored on reload. Unknown ids are a no-op, and a change fires `os-window-changed` with `reason: 'always-on-top'`. While on, the window element carries `os-window--on-top`.
+
 #### Child windows — Stable
 
 A **child window** is a real window — own chrome, drag, resize, minimize, taskbar entry — with one rule layered on top: **its owner can never sit above it.** Clicking the owner hands focus to the child and shakes it rather than raising the owner.
